    "swallow": {
      "description": "The bite. Played once over a mouth that has just taken somebody, and it does not come back open: the teeth clamp in a tenth of a second, give once, and then screw down and in while the gullet constricts and draws after them — which is the shape a swallow actually is. The throat is deliberately not collapsed away to nothing: emptying it leaves the ink lip standing on its own as a big flat hole, which reads as the mouth gone rather than the mouth shut, so what contracts is the flesh and what stays is the edge (`lip_edge`, `wall_shade`, `rim_lit` all hold their place). Linear rather than eased, because a snap that arrives on a curve is not a snap. It ends clamped and holds there, which is the frame the caved-in plug is swapped in on, so the two read as one movement rather than a cut. The lip kicks out once as the teeth close and settles: the ground gets shoved, not the bank.",
